1. Introduction
The demand for freshwater is rapidly outpacing supply, exacerbated by limited raw water allocation, water pollution, and increasing industrial activities [1–3]. This growing imbalance between freshwater supply and demand underscores the urgent need to develop a resilient water supply system. As a result, alternative water sources, such as desalinated seawater and reclaimed wastewater, could offer viable solutions to reduce the reliance on conventional freshwater resources [4–6]. However, seawater desalination presents significant challenges due to its high total dissolved solids (TDS) content, which necessitates substantial energy input for effective treatment. This energy-intensive process not only results in elevated production costs but also raises environmental concerns, such as the management of seawater brine and the increased carbon footprint associated with high energy consumption [7–9]. Therefore, there is a growing shift toward low-TDS wastewater reclamation, which offers a sustainable alternative for water resource management. Municipal wastewater has recently gained attention as an alternative water source, largely because of its low-TDS property. Various technologies have been successfully implemented worldwide to reclaim municipal wastewater as high-quality product water. Among these, membrane-based technologies are considered essential components of municipal wastewater treatment processes [10–12]. The primary membrane separation techniques are differentiated by their selected pore sizes, including microfiltration (MF), ultrafiltration (UF), nanofiltration (NF), and reverse osmosis (RO). MF membranes are primarily utilized for the removal of colloidal particles and suspended solids, whereas UF membranes are proficient in retaining macromolecules, bacteria, protozoa and viruses [12]. NF membranes are employed for the removal of polyvalent ions and dissolved organics [11], and RO membranes are highly effective at retaining nearly all dissolved salts and minerals [13]. For example, in Singapore, RO, combined with MF as a pretreatment step, has been used to treat municipal wastewater, producing high-grade reclaimed water suitable for industrial applications and indirect potable use [14,15]. However, a common limitation of RO is the trade-off between water permeability and salt rejection [16,17]. Therefore, alternative technologies that can achieve both high energy efficiency and high water permeability without compromising salt rejection performance are needed, making them more suitable for municipal wastewater reclamation. In recent years, capacitive deionization (CDI) has garnered attention because of its high energy efficiency and effective salt removal rates when treating low-TDS solutions.
CDI is a promising electro-membrane separation technology that relies on the electrical storage of ions in porous or intercalation materials [18,19]. Compared with conventional CDI systems, the incorporation of ion-exchange membranes (IEMs) onto electrodes in membrane capacitive deionization (MCDI) systems reduces coion expulsion. Consequently, MCDI demonstrates elevated performance in both ion separation and energy efficiency [20,21]. Moreover, MCDI has been applied in various fields, including groundwater desalination, hard water softening, wastewater reclamation, and desalination, which showcases its versatility and effectiveness across a range of water treatment scenarios [22–24]. To date, only a limited number of publications have reported on pilot-scale MCDI systems, particularly in realsite deionization environments. Can ̃as Kurz et al. conducted a pilot-scale MCDI study for brackish groundwater desalination in Essaouira, Morocco [25]. The system achieved a salt removal efficiency of 66%, with a corresponding water recovery of 48%, while operating at a flow rate ranging between 28.0 and 31.6 L/min. Similarly, Zhu et al. conducted a pilot-scale MCDI system with a capacity of 20 CMD for brackish groundwater desalination over 1522.5 h throughout 562 days in the Northern Territory of Australia [22]. The system reduced the salt concentration to approximately 550 mg/L total dissolved solids (TDS) with a water recovery of 72% and treated groundwater with a TDS of 1002.5 mg/L. Both studies incorporated periodic acid cleaning during long-term testing, but the system performance under continuous 24/7 operation was limited. Moreover, continuous water quality monitoring is crucial in determining whether MCDI can serve as an efficient technology for reclaimed water production. Therefore, it is crucial to develop a comprehensive decision framework for MCDI process design and operational optimization to facilitate the successful transition of MCDI into industry-ready technology. A theoretical framework for MCDI desalination plant design was previously proposed, which highlights the interconnections between design parameters, operational conditions, and key performance metrics. However, when the influent and effluent concentrations are fixed at 20 mM and 13 mM, the model does not capture the variability in feedwater quality typically observed in realworld applications. Additionally, it overlooks the potential decline in system performance over extended operational periods, limiting its relevance for long-term use [26]. In this study, a decision framework for the pilot-scale MCDI water reclamation process was successfully developed, providing a structural and functional design for the MCDI system. Following the decision framework, the MCDI-based process was demonstrated for continuous and real-time reclamation of secondary effluent from a domestic
