2. Materials and methods
2.1. Setup of the MCDI unit
The MCDI unit was composed of 12 MCDI stacks arranged in parallel, designed with a standardized configuration to ensure a consistent ion
removal capacity. Each stack housed 32 electrode pairs, separated by a spacer within the 3-mm flow chamber of the MCDI stacks, ensuring the uniform distribution of the feed solution. The electrode, measuring 20 ×
40 cm2 in area, was fabricated by applying activated carbon (AC, ACS20, China Steel Chemical Co., Taiwan) onto titanium current collectors. The cathode was covered by a cation exchange membrane (CEM, C11, Beijing Jie Rui Environmental Science and Technology Corporation, China), while the anion exchange membrane (AEM, A11, Beijing Jie Rui Environmental Science and Technology Co., China) was placed alongside the anode. To apply voltage, all current collectors from both the cathodes and anodes were connected to two separate terminals.
2.2. Commissioning process for pilot-scale MCDI
The pilot-scale MCDI system was housed within a 20-ft standard shipping container situated at the Kerya Water Resources Recycling Center, a DWTP in Hsinchu, Taiwan. The MCDI-based water reclamation process was designed to reclaim secondary effluent treated by a bioreactor at the DWTP. It mainly comprises an SF unit, a UF unit, an MCDI unit, and a UV disinfection unit, as depicted in Fig. 1. The SF and UF (ZW1500, SUEZ, France) units were designed as pretreatment processes to remove foulants and turbidity prior to the MCDI process, aiming to reduce fouling potential and prevent clogging. Following the MCDI unit, the UV (AB-12 A, Aqua Best, China) unit was employed to deter biofouling growth. The feed solution was pumped into the MCDI-based water reclamation process via a water pump (TP320PTB, Walrus Pump Co., Taiwan) equipped with flow meters (H–600P, Hsin Jeng Instrument Co., Taiwan). Level meters were installed to regulate water levels, adjusting pump operation if levels dropped below the margin and preventing overflow by stopping the pump. The programmable logic controller automated the MCDI-based water reclamation process by managing all pumps, valves, power supplies, and meters. Following the construction and assembly process of the pilot-scale MCDI system, thorough site acceptance testing was conducted on every component and the entire MCDI-based water reclamation process, as shown in Fig. 2. To ensure the proper functioning of each component, this testing regimen involves procedures such as pipe flushing, leak testing, and comprehensive electrical testing, among others. All possible malfunctions were identified and resolved before conducting performance testing of the MCDI system.
2.3. Benchmarking procedure for the MCDI unit
After the commission of the pilot-scale MCDI system, the process advanced to proof-of-performance testing, which was conducted within the benchmarking procedure and aligned with the decision framework, as illustrated in Fig. 2. The MCDI performance was analyzed through rigorous testing under constant voltage conditions. The voltages for the charging, regeneration, and discharging steps were set at 2 V, 2 V, and 2 V, respectively. Notably, off-flow regeneration at 2 V was introduced in this study to achieve significantly improved electrode regeneration performance. The feed solution was introduced at a flow rate of 90 LPH in single-pass mode during the on-flow charging and discharging steps, except during the off-flow regeneration step, where the flow rate was set to 0 LPH. The overall treatment capacity reached at least 20 CMD. An ion removal efficiency target of 80% was set for the MCDI-based water reclamation process. During the goal-striving process, testing was divided into two stages: the benchmark procedure and long-term stability assessment. The benchmark procedure focused on two primary criteria: enhancing water treatment capacity and water recovery. The water treatment capacity was examined through the optimization of the charging time (350, 400, 450, and 500 s), whereas the water recovery was controlled through the modification of the discharging time (60, 90, 120, and 150 s). After benchmarking the pilot-scale MCDI performance, long-term stability assessment was conducted under optimized
