3. Results and discussion
3.1. Assessment of the water treatment capacity of the MCDI unit
To increase the water treatment capacity while maintaining an 80% ion removal efficiency, the MCDI unit underwent testing with varying Tc (350, 400, 450, and 500 s) at 2 V. Meanwhile, Tr and Td were fixed at 120 s and 150 s at 2 V, respectively. As shown in Fig. 3 (a), the electrical conductivity (EC) decreased from 1150 to 96 μS/cm because of the electrosorption of ions during the 400-s charging period. After the offflow regeneration step of 120 s, the ions released into the solution were flushed out during the 150-s discharging step at 2 V, resulting in the production of a brine solution. When the Tc increased from 350 to 500 s, the EC of the produced water at the end of the charging step slightly increased from 84 to 168 μS/cm, indicating gradual saturation of the electrode (Fig. 3 (b)). This observation corresponded with the increase in the concentration front from 2218 to 2690 μS/cm during the discharging step. Each charging time underwent 40 consecutive cycles as part of the system performance evaluation, as depicted in Fig. 3 (c). The average normalized concentrations (C/C0) of the MCDI effluent were 0.14, 0.18, 0.23, and 0.27 for Tc values of 350, 400, 450, and 500 s, respectively. These results indicated that the RE decreased from 86% to 73% (Fig. 3 (d)). The DE was confirmed by the SB for both the charging and discharging periods. As illustrated in Fig. 3 (e), although the SB of charging and discharging increased with the Tc, a decrease in DE was observed from 82% to 78%. This decrease in DE was attributed to the higher Tc, leading to elevated ion concentrations remaining in the MCDI unit [18],
