3.2. Assessment of water recovery for the MCDI unit
Considering the critical importance of the water reclamation process in alleviating water scarcity, this study explored the water recovery performance of the MCDI unit. In general, a shorter Td duration corresponds to a higher WR under constant flow rate conditions [27,28]. Fig. 4 illustrates the impact of different Td durations (60, 90, 120, and 150 s) under 2 V on the MCDI system performance. Throughout these variations, Tc was consistently maintained at 400 s under 2 V, and Tr was held constant at 120 s under 2 V. The operating parameter details are depicted in Fig. 4(a). Nevertheless, considering the regeneration capability of the electrode, longer Td durations were necessary to ensure complete flushing of the desorbed ions from the MCDI unit. As depicted in Fig. 4(b), the EC was measured at 1690 μS/cm after a 60-s discharging period, which was 1.8 times greater than that after a 150-s discharging period (936 μS/cm). Incomplete flushing during the discharging step could result in elevated EC values within the MCDI stack, thereby affecting the unit performance in subsequent cycles of MCDI operation. The time-course results of EC concerning the impact of Td are presented in Fig. 4(c). As Td decreased from 150 s to 60 s, the C/C0 of the produced water increased from 0.13 to 0.29, which was consistent with the incomplete flushing of the desorbed ions from the previous cycle. This led to the RE falling below 80% when Td was less than 90 s (Fig. 4 (d)). According to Fig. 4(e), the SB of the charging step remained in the range of 388 to 400 mg owing to the constant Tc. However, there was a declining trend in SB during the discharging step, decreasing from 323 mg with a Td of 150 s to 275 mg with a Td of 60 s. This trend aligns with the high EC remaining in the MCDI stack shown in Fig. 4(b), and the low DE value reflected this phenomenon. Moreover, the duration of the Td plays a critical role in determining WR during MCDI operation under constant flow rate conditions, as a shorter Td improves WR by reducing the volume of brine solution [27,28]. For example, the WR reached 87% when Td was set at 60 s with a 400-s charging period, whereas it decreased to 73% when Td was 150 s (Fig. 4(f)). With respect to the energy performance for the MCDI unit, Ev ranged from 0.29 to 0.30
kWh/m3, except for the condition under 120 s (0.27 kWh/m3), which was attributed to the relatively low influent concentration. The η was within an acceptable range of 76% to 83%, as shown in Fig. 4(g). Overall, a lower Td increased the WR but resulted in a lower RE. To strike a balance between the RE and WR, a Td of 120 s was chosen. This operational condition delivered a high RE of 85% while maintaining a WR of 77%, demonstrating the ability of MCDI system to effectively balance ion removal and water recovery for practical desalination applications.
3.3. Long-term MCDI stability performance with acidic cleaning over 24 weeks
In the context of water reclamation, ion removal efficiency is one of the most critical parameters for ensuring the long-term reliability of pilot-scale MCDI. On the basis of proof-of-performance testing conducted within the benchmarking procedure, the optimized operating
MCDI conditions were selected as follows: charging at 2.0 V for 400 s, regenerating at 2 V for 120 s, and discharging at 2 V for 120 s. The pilot-scale MCDI process was operated for 24 weeks with the goal of achieving 80% ion removal efficiency. During this period, the unit was maintained in continuous 24/7 operation with treatment capacity of 21.1 CMD to assess its operational management. Moreover, according to the literature, mineral scaling and organic fouling are significant challenges in membrane-based separation processes [29,30]. To mitigate these issues, periodic cleaning with citric acid, a mild chelating agent, was applied to the MCDI unit [31]. Fig. 5(a) shows the time-course results of the MCDI unit performance. The average EC of the MCDI influent was 601 ± 41 μS/cm, with the exception of week 18, when it reached 1179 μS/cm. This sudden increase in EC is likely attributable to variations in domestic wastewater quality, which can fluctuate significantly due to the nature of decentralized source pollution. Consequently, even under optimized operating conditions with a constant flow rate of 90 LPH, the EC of the MCDI effluent fluctuated between 40 and 408 μS/cm, except during week 18, when it ranged from 480 to 888 μS/cm. The average daily performance of RE and the frequency of citric acid cleaning (fAC) are shown in Fig. 5 (b). During the first three weeks, citric acid was administered at a frequency of 1.3 times per day. The RE gradually decreased from 91% to 77% and then decreased to 44% when the EC of the influent (701 ± 4 μS/cm) was 17% higher than the average value (601 ± 41 μS/cm). To achieve the target RE of 80%, the fAC was increased from 1.3 times per day to 1.5 times per day. However, MCDI performance deterioration was observed by RE between weeks 3 and 14. Initially, this adjustment led to a surge in RE to 93%, followed by a subsequent decline to 58% by week 14. As a result, the fAC was further increased to 2.25 times per day. Despite the drastic plunge in RE to 40% during week 18, attributed to the soaring EC of the MCDI influent, RE stabilized at approximately 66% between weeks 13 and 17. The removal efficiency (RE) gradually increased from 61% to 81% between weeks 19 and 21. It then consistently remained within the range of 81% to 86% until the end of week 24, successfully meeting the target RE of over 80% (Fig. 2). The high RE indicated a low EC of reclaimed water. Throughout weeks 21 to 24, the average ECs of the MCDI influent and effluent were 516 ± 48 μS/cm and 97 ± 17 μS/cm, respectively. Afterward, the difference in acid dosing frequency affected the RE of the MCDI unit, resulting in fluctuations in water reclamation performance.
3.4. Evaluation of the physic-chemical parameters of the treated water
The physic-chemical parameters were evaluated for effluents from the SF, UF, and MCDI units during both the benchmark procedure and long-term stability assessment stages. The 6 parameters included turbidity, TH, NH3-N, TP, TOC, and COD. During the benchmark procedure, the UF and MCDI units had negligible impact on turbidity, ranging from 0.00 to 0.38 NTU, when treating secondary effluent from DWTP (Fig. S1(a) and S2(a)). Notably, compared with the SF and UF units, the MCDI unit significantly removed charged species. Thus, as depicted in Fig. S1(b) and Fig. S2(b), the average TH was 158.3 ± 21.8 and 157.5 ± 23.2 mg/L as CaCO3 after the SF and UF units, respectively, but decreased to 21.2 ± 6.4 mg/L after the MCDI unit. Notably, the
primary NH3-N species present was NH4+ because of the neutral pH (pH
6.6–6.8) of the effluent. Approximately 67% of the NH4+ was removed after passing through the MCDI unit (as illustrated in Fig. S1(c) and Fig. S2(c)). Similarly, among the phosphorous ions, the dominant P
species were H2PO4 and HPO42 under neutral pH conditions. The average TP concentration in the MCDI effluent was 0.9 ± 0.5 mg/L, which was significantly lower than that in the UF effluent (1.7 ± 1.1 mg/L) (Fig. S1(d) and Fig. S2(d)). Conversely, the non-polar or weakly charged characteristics of organic matter resulted in low removal efficiencies [32,33]. As shown in Fig. S1(e) and Fig. S2(e), the TOC after the UF unit was 4.1 ± 0.8 mg/L and decreased to only 3.6 ± 0.9 mg/L after the MCDI unit, corresponding to a mere 12% removal efficiency. For secondary effluent, the COD/TOC ratios typically range from 2 to 3
